---
title: "Tutorial 4: A phylogenomic workflow with a shell script"
description: Run SEGUL, IQ-TREE, and ASTRAL from one Bash script, from 4,040 UCE alignments to concatenated and species trees.
sidebar:
  order: 4
  label: "4. Workflow with a shell script"
---

This tutorial builds the same workflow as [Tutorial 3](/docs/tutorials/tutorial-3/), as one Bash script instead of a Snakemake workflow. It takes a published set of alignments to two phylogenetic trees with one command. You will:

1. Install the software in one conda environment.
2. Write the script, one step at a time.
3. Run it and check the results.
4. Change a setting and run it again.
5. Submit it as a job on a cluster.

A script needs nothing beyond a shell, and every line is a command you could type yourself. It suits a short, linear analysis. For many samples, jobs spread over cluster nodes, or reruns that skip finished steps, use [Snakemake](/docs/tutorials/tutorial-3/).

## The software

| Software | Role in the workflow |
| --- | --- |
| [Bash](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/) | Runs the steps in order and stops at the first error. |
| [SEGUL](/docs/cli-usage/intro/) | Summarizes, filters, trims, and concatenates the alignments. |
| [IQ-TREE](https://iqtree.github.io/) | Infers a maximum likelihood tree from the supermatrix, and one gene tree per locus. |
| [ASTRAL](https://github.com/chaoszhang/ASTER) | Estimates a species tree from the gene trees. |

## Before you start

- **A computer with Linux or macOS.** On Windows, use [WSL](https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/wsl/install).
- **Conda.** [Miniforge](https://conda-forge.org/download/) is a good way to get it. Check with `conda --version`.
- **About 1 GB of free storage**, and an internet connection for the first run.
- **Basic use of a terminal.** You do not need to know how to write shell scripts.

With 8 cores, the whole workflow takes about 10 minutes.

## The dataset

The data are the same shrew UCE alignments as in [Tutorial 1](/docs/tutorials/tutorial-1/), from [Esselstyn et al. 2021](https://doi.org/10.1206/0003-0090.454.1.1): 4,040 NEXUS alignments of 102 taxa, in a 14.6 MB archive on [Zenodo](https://zenodo.org/records/6459213). The script downloads it.

## The workflow

| Step | Software | Output |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Download and unpack the alignments | curl, unzip | `data/loci` |
| Summarize the raw alignments | SEGUL | `results/01-summary-raw` |
| Keep loci with enough taxa | SEGUL | `results/02-filtered` |
| Trim sites with missing data | SEGUL | `results/03-trimmed` |
| Summarize the clean alignments | SEGUL | `results/04-summary-clean` |
| Concatenate the loci | SEGUL | `results/05-supermatrix` |
| Infer the concatenated tree | IQ-TREE | `results/06-concat-tree` |
| Infer a tree per locus | IQ-TREE | `results/07-gene-trees` |
| Estimate the species tree | ASTRAL | `results/08-species-tree` |

## 1. Create the project

The project is a folder with the script and a file that lists the software:

```plaintext
segul-phylo/
├── environment.yaml
├── run.sh
└── submit.sh      for a cluster run only (step 8)
```

```bash
mkdir segul-phylo
```

```bash
cd segul-phylo
```

Create the files in the next sections with any text editor.

## 2. Install the software

`environment.yaml` lists the conda channels and each package with its version. Fixed versions give the same results on another computer or a year from now. The `aster` package contains ASTRAL.

```yaml title="environment.yaml"
name: segul-phylo
channels:
  - conda-forge
  - bioconda
dependencies:
  - segul=0.25.0
  - iqtree=3.1.4
  - aster=1.25
  - curl
  - unzip
```

Create the environment and activate it:

```bash
conda env create --file environment.yaml
```

```bash
conda activate segul-phylo
```

Check that each program runs:

```bash
segul --version
```

```bash
iqtree --version
```

## 3. Write the script

Add each block below to `run.sh`, in order.

### Settings

The first lines make the script stop at the first error, so a failed step never feeds an empty file to the next one. The settings you may want to change come next, so you never edit the commands themselves.

```bash title="run.sh"
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# From UCE alignments to concatenated and species trees with SEGUL, IQ-TREE, and ASTRAL.

# Stop at the first error, unset variable, or failed command in a pipe.
set -euo pipefail

# Settings
DATASET_URL="https://zenodo.org/records/6459213/files/esselstyn_et_al_2021_uce_aln.zip?download=1"
PREFIX="shrews"      # Name of the supermatrix and the trees.
MIN_TAXA=0.75        # Keep loci with at least this proportion of the taxa.
MAX_MISSING=0.5      # Remove sites where more than this proportion of the taxa is missing.
MODEL="GTR+G"        # Substitution model for IQ-TREE.
IQTREE_OPTIONS="--fast"  # Extra IQ-TREE options. Set to "" for a full search.
THREADS="${THREADS:-8}"  # Cores for IQ-TREE and ASTRAL. 8 unless set when running.

TRIMMED="results/03-trimmed/trimmed_alignments"

step() {
    echo "[$(date +%H:%M:%S)] $1"
}

# Each run starts fresh, so results always match the settings above.
rm -rf results logs
mkdir -p data results logs
```

`THREADS` is 8 unless you set it when you run the script, as in `THREADS=16 ./run.sh`. A cluster job uses this to match the cores it was given. `step` prints the time and the name of each step, so you can follow the run. The script deletes `results` and `logs` when it starts. Keep nothing of your own in them.

### Get the data

```bash title="run.sh"
# 1. Get the data. Skipped when the alignments are already there.
if [ ! -d data/loci ]; then
    step "Downloading the alignments"
    curl --location --silent --show-error --output data/alignments.zip "$DATASET_URL"
    unzip -q -j data/alignments.zip '*.nex' -d data/loci
fi
```

`unzip -j` leaves out the folder inside the archive, so the alignments land in `data/loci`. The download runs once. Later runs reuse the alignments.

### Summarize and filter with SEGUL

```bash title="run.sh"
# 2. Summarize the raw alignments.
step "Summarizing the raw alignments"
segul align summary --dir data/loci --input-format nexus \
    --output results/01-summary-raw --prefix raw --log logs/summary-raw.log

# 3. Keep loci with enough taxa.
step "Filtering loci"
segul align filter --dir data/loci --input-format nexus \
    --percent "$MIN_TAXA" --output results/02-filtered --log logs/filter.log
# SEGUL adds the threshold to the folder name, such as `_75p`.
mv results/02-filtered_*p results/02-filtered
```

- **`--log`** sends SEGUL's log file to the `logs` folder. Without it, every step writes `segul.log` in the project folder.
- **`"$MIN_TAXA"`** passes a setting to the command. Quote variables so a value with spaces stays one argument.

`segul align filter` adds the threshold to the name of its output folder, such as `02-filtered_75p`. The `mv` command gives it a fixed name, so the next step does not depend on the threshold.

### Trim, summarize again, and concatenate

```bash title="run.sh"
# 4. Trim sites with missing data.
step "Trimming sites"
segul align trim --dir results/02-filtered --input-format nexus \
    --missing-data "$MAX_MISSING" --output results/03-trimmed --log logs/trim.log

# 5. Summarize the clean alignments.
step "Summarizing the clean alignments"
segul align summary --dir "$TRIMMED" --input-format nexus \
    --output results/04-summary-clean --prefix clean --log logs/summary-clean.log

# 6. Concatenate the loci.
step "Concatenating loci"
segul align concat --dir "$TRIMMED" --input-format nexus \
    --output-format phylip --partition-format raxml \
    --output results/05-supermatrix --prefix "$PREFIX" --log logs/concat.log
```

`segul align trim` writes the alignments to a `trimmed_alignments` folder inside its output folder, next to `trimming_summary.csv`. The variable `TRIMMED` points to that folder.

### Infer the concatenated tree

```bash title="run.sh"
# 7. Infer the concatenated tree.
step "Inferring the concatenated tree"
mkdir -p results/06-concat-tree
iqtree -s "results/05-supermatrix/$PREFIX.phy" -m "$MODEL" $IQTREE_OPTIONS -T "$THREADS" \
    --prefix "results/06-concat-tree/$PREFIX" --quiet
```

IQ-TREE writes its files with the `--prefix` path but does not create the folder, hence the `mkdir`. `$IQTREE_OPTIONS` has no quotes, so several options split into separate arguments, and an empty value adds none.

### Infer gene trees in parallel

```bash title="run.sh"
# 8. Infer one tree per locus, several at a time.
step "Inferring gene trees"
mkdir -p results/07-gene-trees
gene_tree() {
    local locus
    locus=$(basename "$1" .nex)
    iqtree -s "$1" -m "$MODEL" $IQTREE_OPTIONS -T 1 \
        --prefix "results/07-gene-trees/$locus" --quiet
}
export -f gene_tree
export MODEL IQTREE_OPTIONS
find "$TRIMMED" -name '*.nex' | xargs -P "$THREADS" -I {} bash -c 'gene_tree "$1"' _ {}
```

A `for` loop would infer one gene tree at a time. Instead, `find` lists the trimmed alignments and `xargs -P` runs `gene_tree` on up to `THREADS` of them at once, each with one core. `export` makes the function and the settings visible to the shells that `xargs` starts. If any gene tree fails, `xargs` reports it and the script stops.

### Estimate the species tree

```bash title="run.sh"
# 9. Estimate the species tree from the gene trees.
step "Estimating the species tree"
mkdir -p results/08-species-tree
cat results/07-gene-trees/*.treefile > results/08-species-tree/gene-trees.tre
astral4 -t "$THREADS" -i results/08-species-tree/gene-trees.tre \
    -o "results/08-species-tree/$PREFIX.tre" 2> logs/species-tree.log

step "Done"
```

`cat` joins the gene trees into one file, the input that ASTRAL needs. ASTRAL writes its progress to the error stream, which `2>` sends to the log.

## 4. Check the script

Make the script executable, and let Bash check it for syntax errors without running it:

```bash
chmod +x run.sh
```

```bash
bash -n run.sh
```

No output means no errors. Otherwise, Bash names the line.

## 5. Run the workflow

```bash
./run.sh
```

The script prints each step as it starts, followed by SEGUL's report for that step. The step lines look like this. The SEGUL steps take seconds, and IQ-TREE takes most of the time:

```plaintext
[13:37:31] Downloading the alignments
[13:37:52] Summarizing the raw alignments
[13:37:53] Filtering loci
[13:37:53] Trimming sites
[13:37:54] Summarizing the clean alignments
[13:37:54] Concatenating loci
[13:37:54] Inferring the concatenated tree
[13:43:58] Inferring gene trees
[13:44:43] Estimating the species tree
[13:45:10] Done
```

To keep the run going after you close the terminal, and save its messages, run it with `nohup ./run.sh > run.log 2>&1 &` instead.

{% aside type="tip" title="When a step fails" %}
The script stops at the failed step, and the last line printed names it. SEGUL's own messages are in the `logs` folder. Fix the cause and run the script again. It starts over from the first step, but reuses the downloaded alignments.
{% /aside %}

## 6. Check the results

```plaintext
results/
├── 01-summary-raw/      raw_alignment_summary.txt, locus and taxon tables
├── 02-filtered/         983 alignments
├── 03-trimmed/          trimmed_alignments/, trimming_summary.csv
├── 04-summary-clean/    clean_alignment_summary.txt, locus and taxon tables
├── 05-supermatrix/      shrews.phy, shrews_partition.txt
├── 06-concat-tree/      shrews.treefile
├── 07-gene-trees/       983 gene trees
└── 08-species-tree/     gene-trees.tre, shrews.tre
```

Compare the two summaries:

```bash
head -n 7 results/01-summary-raw/raw_alignment_summary.txt results/04-summary-clean/clean_alignment_summary.txt
```

| | Raw | Clean |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Taxa | 102 | 102 |
| Loci | 4,040 | 983 |
| Sites | 5,398,947 | 756,449 |
| Missing data | 49.43% | 13.55% |

These match [Tutorial 1](/docs/tutorials/tutorial-1/#what-you-did) and [Tutorial 3](/docs/tutorials/tutorial-3/#8-check-the-results). To browse a summary in the terminal, run `segul view summary` on its `alignment-report.json` file. See [viewing summaries](/docs/cli-usage/view-summary/).

The two trees are in Newick format, each with 102 taxa:

- `results/06-concat-tree/shrews.treefile`: the maximum likelihood tree of the supermatrix.
- `results/08-species-tree/shrews.tre`: the ASTRAL species tree, with support values on its branches.

Open them in a tree viewer such as [FigTree](https://github.com/rambaut/figtree/releases) or [iTOL](https://itol.embl.de/).

## 7. Change a setting and rerun

{% steps %}

1. In `run.sh`, change `MIN_TAXA` to `0.8`.
2. Run the script again:

   ```bash
   ./run.sh
   ```

{% /steps %}

The script skips the download and reruns every other step. The stricter filter keeps 308 loci, so this run is faster. Unlike [Snakemake](/docs/tutorials/tutorial-3/#9-change-a-setting-and-rerun), the script does not know which steps a setting affects, so it reruns the raw summary too.

## 8. Run on a cluster

On a cluster with [Slurm](https://slurm.schedmd.com/), submit the script as one job. Add `submit.sh` to the project folder:

```bash title="submit.sh"
#!/usr/bin/env bash
#SBATCH --job-name=segul-phylo
#SBATCH --cpus-per-task=8
#SBATCH --mem=8G
#SBATCH --time=02:00:00
#SBATCH --output=segul-phylo_%j.log

set -euo pipefail

# Make conda available in the job. Clusters set this up differently,
# such as with `module load conda`. Check your cluster's documentation.
eval "$(conda shell.bash hook)"
conda activate segul-phylo

# Use the cores that Slurm gave the job.
THREADS="$SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK" ./run.sh
```

- **`--cpus-per-task`** is the number of cores. The script reads it from `SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK`, so change it in one place only.
- **`--mem`** is the memory for the whole job. The concatenated tree needs about 4 GB.
- **`--time`** is the longest the job may run. Slurm stops it after that.
- **`--output`** saves all messages to a log file. `%j` becomes the job number.

### Settings for your cluster

The header above works on many clusters, but most university HPC centers add their own rules. Common ones are an account or allocation to charge the job to (`--account`), a partition or queue (`--partition`), and limits on memory and time. Check your center's documentation, or ask its support team, before you submit.

For example, [LSU HPC](https://www.hpc.lsu.edu/documentation/job-submission/) requires an allocation, uses the `single` partition for jobs on part of one node, and gives each core 4 GB of memory instead of taking `--mem`:

```bash title="submit.sh (LSU HPC)"
#!/usr/bin/env bash
#SBATCH --job-name=segul-phylo
#SBATCH --account=your_allocation_name
#SBATCH --partition=single
#SBATCH --nodes=1
#SBATCH --cpus-per-task=8
#SBATCH --time=02:00:00
#SBATCH --output=segul-phylo_%j.log
```

Replace `your_allocation_name` with your allocation. The rest of `submit.sh` stays the same. To see the partitions and their time limits, run `sinfo` on a login node.

### Submit the job

Submit the job from the project folder, then check its state:

```bash
sbatch submit.sh
```

```bash
squeue --me
```

When the job ends, read `segul-phylo_<job number>.log` and the `results` folder as in [step 6](#6-check-the-results).

{% aside type="note" title="No internet on compute nodes" %}
Many clusters block downloads from compute nodes. Download the data first on a login node, from the project folder, then submit the job. The script skips the download when `data/loci` exists.
{% /aside %}

```bash
mkdir -p data && curl --location --output data/alignments.zip "https://zenodo.org/records/6459213/files/esselstyn_et_al_2021_uce_aln.zip?download=1"
```

```bash
unzip -q -j data/alignments.zip '*.nex' -d data/loci
```

## Where to go next

{% aside type="caution" title="A first look, not a final analysis" %}
The settings favor speed. `--fast` shortens the tree search, one model is used for all loci, and the concatenated tree has no support values. For a study, set `IQTREE_OPTIONS` to `""` or to the options you need, such as `-B 1000` for ultrafast bootstrap, and choose thresholds from your own summaries.
{% /aside %}

- **Use your own data.** Put your alignments in `data/loci`, and the script skips the download. Change `--input-format` if they are not NEXUS.
- **Add a step.** Add the command where it belongs, with its input from the step before. Every SEGUL command works the same way. See the [CLI guides](/docs/cli-usage/intro/).
- **Use a partitioned model.** The concatenation step already writes `shrews_partition.txt`. Pass it to IQ-TREE with `-p`.
- **Run many jobs on a cluster.** To spread the gene trees over several nodes, use a workflow manager. [Snakemake](/docs/tutorials/tutorial-3/) can submit each step as its own Slurm job.
- **Share the workflow.** `run.sh`, `submit.sh`, and `environment.yaml` are all someone needs to repeat the analysis. Keep them under version control, without `data`, `results`, and `logs`.
